<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second serfdom</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> refers to the tightening of serfdom in central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from the late fifteenth century onwards, at a time when serfdom was declining in western Europe. It was linked to the growth of grain exports to western markets. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> describe how the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szlachta">szlachta</a> nobility bound peasants more and more strictly to the land to profit from the Baltic grain trade.</p></div>
